From: ASRock Rack X570D4I-2T OpenBMC port
Subject: [PATCH] chassis: keep a persistent boot override when the BIOS clears it
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [workaround for AMI BIOS behaviour]

This board's AMI BIOS unconditionally writes Set System Boot Options
parameter 5 with an all-zero payload once it has consumed the boot flags,
whether or not the persistent bit was set. Handled literally that means
validFlag=0 and permanent=0, so the handler calls setBootOneTime(true),
setBootEnable(false) and setBootSource(Default) -- and a Redfish
BootSourceOverrideEnabled=Continuous is gone after one boot.

The failure is quiet and confusing rather than obvious, because the host
keeps its own copy: with the BMC state wiped the machine went on booting into
Setup on the second boot while Redfish reported None/Disabled. It also resets
BootType to Legacy, so the *next* override inherits Legacy and requests a
legacy PXE that has no boot entry on a UEFI-only boot order.

IPMI 28.13 makes the BMC responsible for the lifetime of these flags: the
valid bit is cleared after the next boot only when the flags are not
persistent. Retiring a persistent override is therefore the BMC's job, and a
clear arriving while the configured override is persistent is a request to
decline. Only the exact clear shape is ignored, and only while the override
is persistent, so one-time overrides and genuine host-initiated changes
behave exactly as before.

--- a/chassishandler.cpp
+++ b/chassishandler.cpp
@@ -2253,6 +2253,27 @@
         using namespace chassis::internal;
         using namespace chassis::internal::cache;
 
+        // The AMI BIOS on this board clears parameter 5 after consuming it,
+        // even for a persistent override. Taking that literally would undo a
+        // Continuous BootSourceOverride after its first boot -- observed
+        // directly: the settings object came back BootMode=Regular,
+        // BootSource=Default, BootType=Legacy, Enabled=false, so Redfish then
+        // reported no override while the host went on honouring one.
+        // Retiring persistent flags is the BMC's job under IPMI 28.13, so
+        // decline the clear -- but only the clear, and only while the
+        // configured override actually is persistent. A one-time override's
+        // clear, and any genuine host-initiated change, are unaffected.
+        if (!validFlag && !permanent)
+        {
+            bool oneTimeEnabled = true;
+            if (getBootOneTime(ctx, oneTimeEnabled) == ipmi::ccSuccess &&
+                !oneTimeEnabled)
+            {
+                lg2::info("Ignoring host clear of persistent boot flags");
+                return ipmi::responseSuccess();
+            }
+        }
+
         try
         {
             rc = setBootOneTime(ctx, !permanent);
